[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-341612"] :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & ⌊x⌋\\text{ is even} \\\\ 1,\\, & ⌊x⌋\\text{ is odd},\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)=u(x-2),\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌊x⌋$ is the floor function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/assets/084c469b225d2a4cc4bc148fa3a42fb1501141d1adcd91d8d37999409a8d313f.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the point $x=2,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}h(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}h(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-341642/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "c6b1a7a3-8afa-5e0e-ac81-bc341c292cba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341642/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "e1ac55ce-3f38-521c-a98a-2b1dfe369b40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341642/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" :answer/id #uuid "62406406-6a97-57cd-9bed-52bb3a30ba89" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341642/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer/id #uuid "4af00528-014e-5750-989f-5a966ddbbecf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341642/selection/3a3755feb1a05cec3176c3d2cc45a265049aa4bdd6b69f00387a116663369c21" :answer/id #uuid "7bafdee7-112c-5f2d-b2a6-51e971cbc1dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,g,$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341642/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9780f17b-d59d-5fb5-987b-1cb503eb4e2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-341642/selection/3a3755feb1a05cec3176c3d2cc45a265049aa4bdd6b69f00387a116663369c21" :answer-field/choices ["q-341642/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" "q-341642/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" "q-341642/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" "q-341642/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" "q-341642/selection/3a3755feb1a05cec3176c3d2cc45a265049aa4bdd6b69f00387a116663369c21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-341642" :question/id #uuid "4689ffaf-d7a6-5ea0-9161-3ec67ff19338" :question/math-academy-id "q-341642" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd},\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{5}{2})+u(x-4).\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/assets/d5a497337426dad07096702a3e211f525a959add8cb1fbaaef05fccac26d634b.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ and $x=4,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5/{2}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to 5/{2}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-341642/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5985dfc1-a05b-5ac9-a33d-8398478e252c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341642"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-341645/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" :answer/id #uuid "f4cd65e6-ca79-5c48-bf1a-94cad2c908db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341645/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "e152bfac-5686-591e-998e-12ea0e487194" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341645/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" :answer/id #uuid "19a9c2a4-659a-51c6-8d02-7a7ddfd858d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341645/selection/03748f52aea6637ec4ac8e6115a0813c94904a56cb803618e611dd65fd847541" :answer/id #uuid "2a5b835b-dad5-53f7-b678-f6af10b37644" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,g$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341645/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102" :answer/id #uuid "bc5f2fb4-c974-556f-b4b7-47dc2f39de8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-341645/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "73da814f-3f52-51d9-ac85-be27daa4b0b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-341645/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" :answer-field/choices ["q-341645/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" "q-341645/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" "q-341645/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" "q-341645/selection/03748f52aea6637ec4ac8e6115a0813c94904a56cb803618e611dd65fd847541" "q-341645/selection/54260034644bf2398d955582fcfe14cc95cc4887ad666376368d8327ed5e3102"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-341645" :question/id #uuid "6531c227-dd34-5700-af1b-321b340b5581" :question/math-academy-id "q-341645" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned},\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{4})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{8})+⋯\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/assets/c93cb4cc987ec951236c40a6e961a9eb9f180a594128d4b871692a140faa31e6.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-341645/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5985dfc1-a05b-5ac9-a33d-8398478e252c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-341645"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-394135/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" :answer/id #uuid "eec44f55-5355-5837-9219-b4e4990d8e05" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394135/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" :answer/id #uuid "8bbfd531-cf72-5e56-8119-b4219fdd1c18" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394135/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" :answer/id #uuid "00784f4d-6616-535b-84e9-7f4d6076547a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394135/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" :answer/id #uuid "c8e87d3f-c3c7-53e8-9718-a515e0b6f4cb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394135/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "3c1043e8-53c9-53ab-b8f0-769de56754e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394135/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c4a66b7-2bc6-5bb7-a851-b66aebcb93b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-394135/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" :answer-field/choices ["q-394135/selection/bb292cda762e5b8cdec132334ba43c76a2cfaab9cfffc89bd50f767fb9b03a74" "q-394135/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" "q-394135/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" "q-394135/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" "q-394135/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-394135" :question/id #uuid "28afde2c-2363-58b2-8a4a-6ad2f7d5c5ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-394135" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4x-3, & x<0 \\\\ -2x+5, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,g(x)={x}^{3}-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}},\\,h(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4x-3, & x<0 \\\\ -2x+5, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}4, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{3/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{5{x}^{2/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{10/3},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{7/3}.\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $h(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-394135/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcf6bdc9-3628-5a16-88d6-80be1670c38d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-394135"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-394140/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" :answer/id #uuid "99df81c7-e869-53e9-a4d3-cbfd86a58248" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394140/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" :answer/id #uuid "31fb7be0-180e-504f-8d06-3aa5a1116421" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394140/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" :answer/id #uuid "ea459030-c213-5460-b5eb-54fc7402fde1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394140/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" :answer/id #uuid "ab6e0fc0-e425-55fb-aa7f-0e072c7b3e07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394140/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e" :answer/id #uuid "e870e5ab-e5a5-522c-b366-9cda833599b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-394140/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4da940ed-b4fa-54dd-b083-ae0219199536" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-394140/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" :answer-field/choices ["q-394140/selection/0c1ca63c3c6c805ef06c7bcf31ae0d5219aad8d8f314916c203a48f8e14c7d02" "q-394140/selection/c820fc75b83ab642062caecaa665e20c3efdace607db6b590468cae51c449859" "q-394140/selection/2a1d8a89f00ace1b1ddd51d9e5ff705929e76b6edeee97f394d666550954368a" "q-394140/selection/9af10456e8aaa200b7ffe625b2da3a488db9dd53569041e400767aa22135d465" "q-394140/selection/8179e352607fde1d20643aaf2dd5550f91f25ca3d0bbbef167f92cf9dc7a168e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-394140" :question/id #uuid "344f57b8-c115-5f45-be4c-e3f3ba12bb83" :question/math-academy-id "q-394140" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4x-1, & x<0 \\\\ 4x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,g(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}},\\,h(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4x-1, & x<0 \\\\ 4x+3, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}4, & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{2}+{x}^{4/3},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=2x+\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{1/3}.\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $g(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{14/5},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{14}{5}{x}^{9/5}.\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $h(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,$ $g,$ and $h.$\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-394140/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcf6bdc9-3628-5a16-88d6-80be1670c38d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-394140"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
